1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,672
This rehearsal joins the school Week Five requirements into a sequence
you can explain and practise. It does not add another hidden module or
substitute a set of disconnected textbook facts for the weekend.

2
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:28,872
Saturday centers on the arrest team, shockable and nonshockable
rhythms, CO2 change, ROSC and resuscitation decisions. Sunday moves
into the deteriorating cardiac patient, timed ECG, perfusion and live
notification.

3
00:00:28,872 --> 00:00:41,304
Use a manikin, training device or spoken tabletop exercise as
appropriate. This course cannot certify physical compression quality,
device fit or a clinical skill from a screen alone.

4
00:00:41,304 --> 00:00:52,536
Practise the original scenarios here, not a school quiz or an answer
key. The purpose is to make the reasoning and teamwork executable
before the actual supervised class.

5
00:00:52,536 --> 00:01:06,600
Use AP and cardiovascular pathophysiology to explain the pump, rhythm
and serial circuit. CNS and MSN connect those mechanisms to the
focused assessment, shock and the nitrate-preload problem.

6
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:19,800
Use BCEHS for the named service pathways and BCPEG for the saved
examination-guide wording. They are not always identical, and a school
page reference does not prove which revision will govern a future
assessment.

7
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:38,712
FON, CPSC and VSM support team behavior, focused observations and safe
practical delivery. PHARM explains the drug mechanisms and risks,
while PMHN provides the ethical concepts relevant to patient wishes
and difficult resuscitation conversations.

8
00:01:38,712 --> 00:01:53,424
Nancy remains the supplied main examination text, integrated through
the weekly topics. The eleven assigned reading rows all have bilingual
guides, but unresolved edition and linked-activity gaps are not marked
fully certified.

9
00:01:53,424 --> 00:02:05,976
Name who is leading, compressing, managing the airway and operating
the monitor. Add treatment, recording and communication roles as staff
arrive, matching tasks to actual capability.

10
00:02:05,976 --> 00:02:17,424
The leader states the immediate goal and prepares the next transition
before it happens. Everyone should know the next planned rhythm
assessment, compressor exchange and relevant safety check.

11
00:02:17,424 --> 00:02:30,960
Use a directed instruction, acknowledgement and completion report. A
read-back is useful communication, but it does not replace observing a
real all-clear or independently checking a calculation when required.

12
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:43,440
Maintain shared awareness of the patient, not only individual tasks.
Anyone who sees ineffective care, a hazard or a major patient change
should state it clearly and receive a response.

13
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:56,664
With limited personnel, prioritize the effective initial arrest
response and use available help. Do not create an elaborate six-person
role plan before compressions and the usable AED pathway begin.

14
00:02:56,664 --> 00:03:10,848
As resources arrive, transfer tasks explicitly: who takes over, what
has happened and what must continue. A working airway or
defibrillation setup should not disappear because someone assumed
another person had it.

15
00:03:10,848 --> 00:03:23,640
Prepare an equipment exchange before removing a working device. A new
monitor or mechanical compressor is not inherently more valuable than
uninterrupted effective treatment already in progress.

16
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:35,760
The team structure can change while the clinical goal stays
consistent. Review any avoidable no-treatment interval afterward and
identify its exact cause rather than blaming the number of people.

17
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:52,416
On the adult manikin, practise the trained compression target of one
hundred to one hundred twenty per minute, approximately five to six
centimeters deep, with full recoil. Device feedback or a displayed
rate is useful but not the entire quality assessment.

18
00:03:52,416 --> 00:04:05,736
Use the appropriate ventilation strategy for the actual airway and
workflow. Avoid excessive rate or volume and check chest movement and
the team’s timing instead of squeezing a full bag on every breath.

19
00:04:05,736 --> 00:04:19,344
Plan necessary pauses and keep them as short as possible; a ten-second
upper bound is not a desired pause length. Count the actual
interruption, including an unplanned delay before compressions resume.

20
00:04:19,344 --> 00:04:31,992
If the manikin reports a problem, change one specific action and
measure again. A subjective feeling of good CPR or a successful story
ending cannot certify the underlying performance.

21
00:04:31,992 --> 00:04:43,968
Begin the drill during ongoing CPR and announce the coming assessment.
Prepare the actual authorized monitor mode, connections and energy
without adding avoidable interruption.

22
00:04:43,968 --> 00:04:55,128
Interpret the rhythm during a reliable brief assessment. Compression
artifact is not a reason to guess a shockable pattern, and a charged
device does not create an indication.

23
00:04:55,128 --> 00:05:07,296
Perform the actual all-clear rather than merely saying a phrase.
Include patient contact, surrounding hazards and the specific oxygen
arrangement under the trained device workflow.

24
00:05:07,296 --> 00:05:19,800
After an indicated shock, promptly resume useful CPR through the
correct workflow. Do not add a routine immediate postshock pulse hunt
or stand watching the screen to see whether the story improved.

25
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:31,608
Change the scenario to organized electrical activity without effective
circulation. That is not a reason to shock merely because the QRS
looks dramatic or the device is charged.

26
00:05:31,608 --> 00:05:43,560
Announce the nonshockable decision, disarm safely if necessary and
resume the appropriate CPR and ventilation sequence. Preserve the
clinical reason and time in the record.

27
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:54,000
Look for plausible reversible causes using the patient’s context and
observations. Do not spend a prolonged pause listing causes while no
one compresses.

28
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:05,616
An AED no-shock message does not prove effective circulation or
authorize stopping resuscitation. Reassess the actual patient and
follow the appropriate arrest decision pathway.

29
00:06:05,616 --> 00:06:20,328
Recall the full school Hs and Ts framework, then attach evidence.
Hypoxia, hypovolemia, hydrogen-ion disturbance, potassium disturbance
and hypothermia are not diagnosed merely by naming them.

30
00:06:20,328 --> 00:06:34,320
The T group includes tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins and
pulmonary or coronary thrombosis. Several mechanisms can coexist; an
arrest rhythm alone does not identify which one is present.

31
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:50,712
Assign a focused question or examination while effective arrest care
continues. A history of fluid loss, a disconnected airway or a
relevant medication exposure can direct a specific correction more
usefully than an unprioritized list.

32
00:06:50,712 --> 00:07:04,008
Some definitive treatments require advanced or specialized resources.
Recognizing the possibility helps escalation, but does not grant an
untrained operator permission to perform an invasive procedure.

33
00:07:04,008 --> 00:07:17,688
Start with a real repeated waveform and understand the sampling
interface. CO2 must be produced, carried to the lungs, exhaled and
sampled, so a number can change for more than one reason.

34
00:07:17,688 --> 00:07:31,224
For a low or falling value, check actual ventilation, airway, sampling
and compression performance in context. Do not automatically ventilate
faster or assume the patient is beyond help.

35
00:07:31,224 --> 00:07:46,344
The school emphasizes a sudden sustained rise as a major ROSC clue.
Communicate it and assess circulation appropriately without an
unnecessary prolonged pause; confirm the transition rather than
declaring it from one number.

36
00:07:46,344 --> 00:08:00,960
For resuscitation decisions, isolated ETCO2 is not a stand-alone
termination test, particularly with nonintubated sampling. Keep the
actual pathway, clinical evidence and consultation requirements
together.

37
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:14,208
Once evidence supports ROSC, announce the new state so the whole team
changes the plan together. Compressions-generated pulses or an
organized trace alone cannot establish the transition.

38
00:08:14,208 --> 00:08:28,608
Reassess airway, breathing, circulation and consciousness. Provide the
indicated support and prepare for recurrent deterioration; the
resuscitation is not finished because one cycle ended with a pulse.

39
00:08:28,608 --> 00:08:48,048
Titrate oxygen when reliable measurements and the applicable pathway
permit, and avoid excessive ventilation. The current N Zero Three
page, its older checklist and the AHA comparison have numerical
differences documented in the preceding lesson; do not fabricate a
blended target.

40
00:08:48,048 --> 00:09:03,552
Obtain the appropriate ECG and cause assessment, coordinate the
receiving pathway and maintain repeated observations. Do not replace
this with indiscriminate cold fluids or a universal prognosis from the
early neurologic examination.

41
00:09:03,552 --> 00:09:15,168
Distinguish not starting CPR, stopping CPR after it has begun and the
subsequent recognition-of-life-extinct process. They are related but
not interchangeable decisions.

42
00:09:15,168 --> 00:09:32,088
Use the actual R Zero Two criteria card from the prior lecture. Rapid,
twenty-minute and thirty-or-forty-minute pathways have conditions and
minimums, not automatic stop alarms. A prior brief ROSC or a shockable
period can change a branch.

43
00:09:32,088 --> 00:09:47,064
Prepare the required early consultation with true no-flow, CPR,
rhythm, shocks, ROSC, causes and patient-wish information. Do not wait
until a timer expires to discover that the important history is
missing.

44
00:09:47,064 --> 00:10:02,592
After an authorized discontinuation, use the actual ROLE process and
required paired observations. A fixed pupil, family preference,
isolated CO2 value or classroom minimum alone does not replace that
process.

45
00:10:02,592 --> 00:10:15,360
Use clear, compassionate language and identify who can communicate
while the clinical team continues care. Do not make promises about
survival or imply that a single monitor value determines the outcome.

46
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:30,000
Ask about known wishes and relevant documents without treating every
relative as the automatic decision-maker. Apply the actual legal and
clinical process, including clarification when documents or accounts
conflict.

47
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:42,360
If resuscitation ends through the authorized process, state the
reality plainly and allow questions. Do not use vague phrases that
leave the family believing treatment has merely paused.

48
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:57,888
Respect privacy and dignity, explain practical next steps through the
appropriate service and document important communication factually.
Ethical principles guide the process; they do not provide blanket
immunity or unilateral TOR authority.

49
00:10:57,888 --> 00:11:11,808
The school names a LUCAS station, while the current public service
procedure allocates mechanical CPR to advanced crews and selected
medical-arrest situations. Learning the station does not erase that
operational boundary.

50
00:11:11,808 --> 00:11:25,992
Use the actual trained device version and IFU. Prepare components and
a manual fallback, apply the correct landmarks and height without
precompression, and verify the state and fit rather than forcing the
frame.

51
00:11:25,992 --> 00:11:39,144
Preserve manual compressions whenever possible during preparation and
keep the necessary application interruption short. A supposedly
flawless machine is not a reason to stop checking effective care.

52
00:11:39,144 --> 00:11:53,208
After attachment or movement, recheck position, mode, actual
compression performance and airway/ventilation. A correctly named mode
alone cannot prove the cup is still in the correct place.

53
00:11:53,208 --> 00:12:05,592
Introduce an unsafe fit or a simulated fault. The expected response is
prompt effective manual CPR and safe device management, not a long
discussion while compressions are absent.

54
00:12:05,592 --> 00:12:19,320
For the planned battery change, the reviewed IFU preserves the
position within its specified interval and returns from PAUSE to
PAUSE. The sixty-second memory is not permission for a sixty-second
CPR interruption.

55
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:33,336
Verify the state and patient before restarting. Use the trained
rhythm-analysis and defibrillation workflow rather than assuming
mechanical motion cannot create artifact or that every procedure
requires the same pause.

56
00:12:33,336 --> 00:12:46,656
After ROSC, do not apply a universal retain-the-frame rule from a
school claim. Patient access, breathing, comfort, position, readiness
and local instruction must inform the plan.

57
00:12:46,656 --> 00:12:58,992
The technical goal is a useful recording, while the clinical goal is
to preserve necessary care. Divide tasks so speed does not come from
ignoring the patient or guessing electrode positions.

58
00:12:58,992 --> 00:13:11,256
Use the verified Week Three contact-to-readable clock as a carryover
practice target. Week Five requests timed acquisition, but an
unrecovered separate grading rule is not invented.

59
00:13:11,256 --> 00:13:23,112
Check patient identity, actual standard lead positions, artifact and
the symptom state. Label additional right-sided or posterior views
distinctly through the trained pathway.

60
00:13:23,112 --> 00:13:33,456
Record the start, usable finish and reason for delay. Practise the
specific weak link again instead of simply trying to move faster
through every action.

61
00:13:33,456 --> 00:13:44,832
Identify the inferior leads as a group and relate the pattern to
symptoms. Do not claim that the same artery or right-ventricular
physiology is proven in every patient.

62
00:13:44,832 --> 00:13:57,912
State the pressure, rate and perfusion rather than simply saying
inferior means no nitrate. The school hypotensive, bradycardic
presentation already fails important current screening conditions.

63
00:13:57,912 --> 00:14:12,144
Ask the relevant medication and volume questions and consider right-
sided involvement. Explain how additional venodilation could reduce
serial flow and why a fluid bolus is not an automatic opposite answer.

64
00:14:12,144 --> 00:14:26,280
Describe the positive care plan after withholding: support threats,
obtain appropriate resources, reassess and coordinate the receiving
pathway. Safe exclusion must lead to useful care, not paralysis.

65
00:14:26,280 --> 00:14:35,424
Open with the time-critical working problem and the patient’s current
state. The receiving team needs more than the words possible cardiac
patient.

66
00:14:35,424 --> 00:14:51,288
Give the important times, ECG territory and perfusion trend. Report
uncertainty honestly, including an unknown onset or artifact
limitation, rather than making the message sound complete by inventing
a fact.

67
00:14:51,288 --> 00:15:04,416
State what treatment occurred and the response, including an important
withheld nitrate and its reason. Request or confirm the actual
regional pathway rather than promising universal ED bypass.

68
00:15:04,416 --> 00:15:16,392
Finish with arrival time, support needs and confirmation of receipt or
the next step. Then continue patient care; a polished radio message
does not guarantee a stable journey.

69
00:15:16,392 --> 00:15:28,392
Take one example from this week: ASA one hundred sixty versus one
hundred sixty-two, or the three different atropine starting doses in
Nancy, PHARM and the checked local monograph.

70
00:15:28,392 --> 00:15:41,880
Name the source and context first. Do not average doses, combine
repeat schedules or select the most permissive number. An educational
advanced-dose comparison is not independent PCP authority.

71
00:15:41,880 --> 00:15:53,184
Explain the physiological purpose and relevant harm. This lets you
understand why the discrepancy matters instead of storing two
unexplained numbers that can be confused under stress.

72
00:15:53,184 --> 00:16:05,664
For an actual school assessment or clinical procedure, obtain the
governing version and instruction through the correct channel. A
course production gap remains visible until that evidence is obtained.

73
00:16:05,664 --> 00:16:18,312
In a training calculation, one milligram divided by point-one
milligram per milliliter equals ten milliliters. The units cancel to
volume, which is why concentration must be explicit.

74
00:16:18,312 --> 00:16:30,648
The same mass from one milligram per milliliter occupies one
milliliter. This arithmetic difference does not make the two products
or routes interchangeable, and it is not a patient-specific order.

75
00:16:30,648 --> 00:16:44,808
For a weight-based infusion, retain kilograms, micrograms, minutes and
hours until the expression ends in milliliters per hour. Check device
precision and the maximum or titration instruction before measuring.

76
00:16:44,808 --> 00:16:57,720
Then check indication, contraindications, route, rate, order and
operator authority. An independently correct calculation can still
describe an unsafe or unauthorized treatment.

77
00:16:57,720 --> 00:17:09,888
Begin with a community-workshop collapse, bystander CPR and a working
AED. Assign roles and preserve the usable care while learning the
reported collapse and no-flow history.

78
00:17:09,888 --> 00:17:22,872
Now introduce a cable fault in the replacement monitor. Assign focused
troubleshooting while the existing CPR and AED workflow continue; do
not remove the working treatment to make room for a broken
replacement.

79
00:17:22,872 --> 00:17:35,664
Later introduce a sustained CO2 rise. The team must communicate, check
the patient appropriately and confirm whether the transition is real,
then use the correct subsequent pathway.

80
00:17:35,664 --> 00:17:50,040
Run a second version with no ROSC and prepare an evidence-based
consultation without inventing an ending. Success is coherent care and
a defensible decision process, not forcing every scenario to survive.

81
00:17:50,040 --> 00:18:01,584
Start with the original chest-symptom patient from the previous
lecture. State the working problem, screen treatment, prepare a useful
ECG and begin the time-sensitive pre-alert.

82
00:18:01,584 --> 00:18:13,992
Change only ventilation to inadequate while a pulse remains. The team
must notice, announce and respond rather than continuing a medication
discussion or automatically choosing CPAP.

83
00:18:13,992 --> 00:18:25,920
Then remove effective circulation. Announce the arrest and allocate
CPR, ventilation and the appropriate analysis workflow, preserving
what remains useful from the existing setup.

84
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:39,168
If the drill later produces confirmed ROSC, announce it and change the
whole plan again. The recorder and communicator must update the
current state, not keep reporting the initial presentation.

85
00:18:39,168 --> 00:18:51,744
Record true times and distinguish observation from witness report or
estimate. No-flow before CPR and low-flow during CPR are different
intervals and may affect later decisions.

86
00:18:51,744 --> 00:19:03,528
Reconcile device events with the actual clinical record. Not every
drug, pulse check, conversation or human decision is automatically
captured, and device clocks can differ.

87
00:19:03,528 --> 00:19:17,064
Document actual interventions and response, consultation facts and
direction, exclusions and unresolved uncertainty. Do not chart an
action as completed merely because it was planned or ordered.

88
00:19:17,064 --> 00:19:29,880
Handover the current patient and required ongoing support using the
trained structure. Confirm transfer of responsibility; the record is
for continuity of care, not a promise of immunity.

89
00:19:29,880 --> 00:19:41,880
Review what actually happened before discussing what should have
happened. Use the interruption clock, manikin feedback, trace quality,
communication and record to identify a specific gap.

90
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:54,432
Do not judge the whole team by ROSC alone or attribute every problem
to an individual. Consider setup, roles, environment, device
compatibility and the information available at the time.

91
00:19:54,432 --> 00:20:07,656
Choose one change: prepare a connector earlier, name the next
compressor before the pause, or include the withheld-nitrate reason in
the radio message. Repeat the same drill to see whether the change
helped.

92
00:20:07,656 --> 00:20:20,064
Separate operational review from personal psychological support. Offer
appropriate voluntary support without forcing traumatic disclosure or
pretending a learning discussion eliminates accountability.

93
00:20:20,064 --> 00:20:31,848
You should now be able to explain why each action belongs in the
sequence, not merely recite a checklist. Link physiology, patient
findings, treatment choice and reassessment.

94
00:20:31,848 --> 00:20:45,480
Practical readiness also requires supervised performance on the actual
equipment and patient-appropriate technique. Watching a lecture and
passing an original practice question cannot establish that by itself.

95
00:20:45,480 --> 00:20:56,904
Keep exact school-edition and missing-PowerPoint or rubric gaps
visible. The course does not mark an unseen requirement complete or
present this rehearsal as a school-assessed pass.

96
00:20:56,904 --> 00:21:08,904
Use the original review questions to identify what to repeat, then
continue into the next school week. The final whole-course package is
withheld until content and delivery checks are complete.
